Repair the following Java 7 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.
Print your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:
<repaired_code>
<your_repaired_code>
</repaired_code>
<confidence>
<your_confidence_score>
</confidence>
Do not provide any other text or markdown fences.

Here is the problem description:
<problem_description>
Vanya has a scales for weighing loads and weights of masses w0, w1, w2, ..., w100 grams where w is some integer not less than 2 (exactly one weight of each nominal value). Vanya wonders whether he can weight an item with mass m using the given weights, if the weights can be put on both pans of the scales. Formally speaking, your task is to determine whether it is possible to place an item of mass m and some weights on the left pan of the scales, and some weights on the right pan of the scales so that the pans of the scales were in balance.
</problem_description>

Here is the Java 7 code you have to repair:
<original_code>
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class CodeForce308C {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        String[] input = in.readLine().split("\\s+");
        long w = Long.parseLong(input[0]);
        long m = Long.parseLong(input[1]);

        if (w == 2) {
            System.out.println("YES");
            return;
        }

        Set<Long> combinationSet = new HashSet<>();
        combinationSet.add(1L);

        for (int i = 0; i <= 100; i++) {
            long power = 1;
            try {
                power = Math.multiplyExact(1L, (long) Math.pow(w, i));
            } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
                break;
            }
            if (power > (long) 2 * Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                break;
            }

            Set<Long> newSet = new HashSet<>();
            for (Long val : combinationSet) {
                newSet.add(val + power);
            }
            combinationSet.addAll(newSet);
            combinationSet.add(power);
        }

        List<Long> combination = new ArrayList<>(combinationSet);
        Collections.sort(combination);

        if (Collections.binarySearch(combination, m) >= 0) {
            System.out.println("YES");
            return;
        }

        for (Long element : combination) {
            if (Collections.binarySearch(combination, element + m) >= 0) {
                System.out.println("YES");
                return;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("NO");
    }
}
</original_code>
